WEBVTT

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.720
Come ride the little train

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:08.420
that is rolling down the tracks to the junction.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.500
Forget about your cares,

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:15.120
it is time to relax at the junction.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:19.870
Lots of curves, you bet,

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:25.060
even more when you get to the junction.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:27.590
Code Junction.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.240
There's a little hotel called a Shady Rest at the Junction.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.470
Petticoat Junction.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:41.950
It is run by Kate. Come and be her guest at the Junction.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:43.560
Petticoat.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:48.630
And that's Uncle Joe. He's a-movin' kinda slow at the Junction.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:50.840
Petticoat Junction.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:15.340
water tower again weren't you yes someday that train's gonna sneak in drain out all the water

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:23.030
before you can get out and leave you high dry and bare mom it's hot where else can we swim don't

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:28.040
worry about that girls i got plans for a big swimming pool we're gonna put it right out here

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.970
in front uh swimming pool

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:31.700
Olympic-sized, Kate.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.080
And in the wintertime, we'll turn it into a skating rink.

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.550
It'll give the hotel class.

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:39.050
Uncle Joe, with business as slow as it is,

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:40.790
we're losing money now.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:42.730
I know we are, but with the swimming pool

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:44.370
and the skating rink, we can raise the rates.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.040
Going to Hooterville, Kate?

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:51.470
Give you a free ride.

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.010
Oh, yeah, Charlie, be right.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.250
What's the matter? You hear something?

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.330
The cannonballs are wheezing this morning.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:01:59.690
Everything all right, Charlie?

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.200
Yeah, she's just getting a little old like the rest of us.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:06.400
I wonder what kind of engines they're running on the main line now.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.670
I wouldn't know, Floyd. It must be 20 years since we've seen the main line.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.280
Yeah, you reckon they'll ever put that trestle back up so we can get there?

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:20.120
No, Floyd, if you ask me, the folks have forgotten all about us back at the CNFW.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.980
This supercharged diesel locomotive is now standard equipment on the entire CNFW.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.030
and fw system five short years ago you brought me out here from the east to do a job for you

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:43.660
well gentlemen i think you'll agree that today we have the most modern the most progressive the most

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:59.580
and aware for what the main line check into it fast

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.200
You charter a plane to this town of Hooterville and ride that branch to the end of the line.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.720
I want a complete report right away.

00:03:06.970 --> 00:03:12.310
Well, gentlemen, Mr. Bedlow here is flying out to Hooterville to look into a branch line.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:13.180
Morning, boys.

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:14.170
Hi, Charlie.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:14.930
Hi, Charlie.

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:16.450
Sorry to keep you waiting.

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:17.710
Well, it's all right.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:18.870
We're in no hurry.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:23.560
Kate, should I put on my conductor suit, or is it just home folks getting on?

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.380
Just home folks, Floyd.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.360
Well, in that case...

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:29.080
Oh

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:34.970
Drop us off at Drucker's door, will you, Charlie?

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:35.450
All right.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:35.870
Come on, girl.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.920
Hey, Charlie, I'd go along, too,

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.440
but I'm afraid with one more passenger,

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.720
the old Hooterville meatball couldn't make the grade.

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:45.580
That's cannonball.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:47.080
Come on, Charlie.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.340
Kate's in a hurry to get to Hooterville.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:50.120
She is?

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.870
In that case, I better go tell her to get off of this thing

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:53.590
and start walking.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.840
Don't pay any attention to him, Charlie.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:35.610
Hey, they didn't stop. They overshot the station.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.620
Oh, they didn't. They'll stop down at the general store.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:41.990
General store, but the station's up here.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:45.580
Yeah, but they're letting Kate and her girls off to do their shopping.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:48.440
Who in the blue blazes is Kate?

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:51.230
Runs the hotel between here and Pixley.

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:53.900
Well, you must be from a long ways off.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.160
You ain't never heard of Kate Bradley and her girls.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.000
Hello, Herbie.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:01.640
Hi, Herbie.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:02.660
Hi, Bobby.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:04.260
Hi, Herbie.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:05.420
Hi, Billy.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.820
Herbie, we don't want to keep the train waiting too long.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:11.080
Yes, Mrs. Bradley.

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:13.760
I've already got some of your regular order ready in the back.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:15.040
We figured you'd begin today.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:15.900
Come on, girls.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:16.520
Let's get busy.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:20.920
Are they just gonna park there and wait while that woman does their shopping?

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:22.100
They usually do.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:24.130
The boys are real fond of Kate.

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.830
But what do the boys think they're operating?

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:28.610
A train or a taxi?

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:29.970
Well, a little...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:30.880
Of both, I reckon.

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.430
When I get through, it won't be either one.

00:05:37.290 --> 00:05:39.270
I'll melt that thing down for paperweights.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:47.430
Floyd and Charlie must be tired of waiting.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:49.510
Let's get the rest of the supplies out to the platform.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:51.190
I'll go see if Billy needs any help.

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.500
Mom, can we get some new swimming suits?

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.280
No, we cannot. Summer's over and put them back.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:05:58.880
I'll help you, Billy.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:05:59.970
Come on, girl.

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:20.940
Sorry, Kate, but them traveling salesmen we picked up are anxious to get to Pixley.

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:24.780
Yeah, sure, sorry to rush you, Kate.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:27.840
Worked out fine, Charlie. Got all our stuff and done.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:28.360
Good.

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:33.710
Let's get the baggage aboard.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:34.990
Don't bother about it, ladies.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:36.930
Floyd and I will put her on there for you.

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:37.630
They can help a little.

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:44.930
When they get their supplies loaded,

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:46.700
are they going to back up that train for me?

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:48.700
Wouldn't count on it.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.160
They don't even know you're here.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:51.400
Well, then, by Sunday,

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:53.220
you're going to hightail it down that track and tell them.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:55.240
I wouldn't count on that either.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:06:58.030
What's your name?

00:06:58.750 --> 00:06:59.590
Sam Drucker.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:00.560
What's yours?

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:01.500
Homer Bedlow.

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:02.790
And you're fired.

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:05.600
There's another thing I wouldn't count on.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.900
Mr. Drucker, I happen to run this railroad

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:10.660
and you're no longer working for it.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:11.810
Never did.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:14.420
I happen to run the general store.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:16.240
What the devil are you doing here?

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:17.340
Why aren't you down at your store?

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:18.430
Well, I tell you.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:20.690
You see, Kate ain't got the money to pay

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.450
for all them supplies she just bought.

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:25.030
And it's a heap less embarrassing for her

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:26.260
if I ain't there.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:28.840
Well, if this isn't a nothingist.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:32.470
Looks like that'll do it, Floyd.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:37.220
Well, best we get rolling now.

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:39.530
Benny Jo, would you like to take the throttle?

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:40.410
Can I, Mom?

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:42.540
You always do going home.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:46.420
And you have a good head of steam, Charlie.

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.320
I'll have to highball it to make the hotel in time for supper.

00:07:49.540 --> 00:07:49.720
Right.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:52.260
Take it easy on dead man's curb.

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:54.180
Billy Jo.

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:55.180
In a minute, Mom.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:56.700
Bobby Jo?

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:58.100
I'm in here, Mom.

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:05.490
He yelled all aboard.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:08.160
Yeah, if you want to cash your train,

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.300
you'd better start shanking it down your track.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.060
I'll melt the whole works down for paperweights.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:18.340
Stop trains!

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:52.170
I've got him down for a cannonball.

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:06.620
Where's your sister?

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:08.050
You mean Billy?

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:10.480
Oh, who else would I mean?

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:12.870
Well, I think she's back there, Mom.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:16.980
Back there, all right.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:22.780
Excuse me, excuse me.

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:26.890
I want to talk to my daughter about the menu for my hotel.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.320
Train gets there just about supper time.

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:29.980
She and her sister...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.740
wait table in the dining room.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:33.680
Sisters? You got more like her?

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:36.580
Oh, three in the hall.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.700
Uh, Billy Joe, I think this evening we'll have fried chicken,

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:42.910
mashed potatoes with cream gravy,

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:45.680
corn on the cob with fresh churned butter,

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:47.420
hot biscuits and jam,

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:50.020
fresh apple pie with homemade ice cream,

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:53.970
and, um, tea. What else?

00:09:54.610 --> 00:09:57.130
Does the train stop long enough for us to eat at your hotel?

00:09:57.580 --> 00:09:59.800
Oh, I think it can be a rain.

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:03.480
But the safe thing to do is plan to spend the night.

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:07.720
You'll never get up to a better breakfast than at the shady rest.

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:09.000
Come on, little Jeff.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:22.270
The railroad signals ass.

00:10:22.410 --> 00:10:24.930
Dead man's curve. You better grab hold of something and...

00:10:32.330 --> 00:10:35.510
I follow that locomotive up there, is either drunk or crazy.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:39.040
Thank you not to talk like that about my little girl.

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:41.960
Your little girl's running this locomotive?

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:44.770
She always does on the way back from town.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:46.640
She and Charlie just like this.

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:47.950
Who's Charlie?

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:48.890
The engineer.

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:53.520
Look, scoot over. It's hard to talk across the aisle.

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:02.810
You one of those reporters?

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:04.450
In a way, yes.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:09.770
Well, Charlie Pratt's the best engineer that ever popped ahead of Steve.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:12.040
And he's letting your child drive this locomotive?

00:11:12.220 --> 00:11:16.190
He's been driving it since he was knee-high to a cowcatcher.

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:26.580
And so much black smoke.

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:28.820
The floor's been burning railroad tires again.

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:34.140
Charlie, take a look. All the pressure I can get is 135.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:36.960
Well, that'll hold you. Don't push her too hard.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:40.990
You know what I think? The cannonball needs a boiler wash.

00:11:41.230 --> 00:11:43.810
Yeah, I reckon she's got some mud in her belly all right.

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:47.730
Let's wash her out when we get to the hotel.

00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:50.660
And I can clean the grates and ash pad at the same time.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:55.080
Betty Jo, you're getting to an age where the boys have got their eye on you,

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:57.880
and it just ain't the most romantic thing in the world

00:11:57.880 --> 00:11:59.980
for the love of your life to come crawling out...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:01.260
out of a locomotive boiler.

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:06.440
As far as I'm concerned, Billy Joe can have the boys.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:10.460
I'm in love with the Hooterville Cannonball.

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:13.720
Well, that's fine for now, hon, but one of these days,

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:16.620
you're gonna want to be hugging something that can hug you back.

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:50.680
joe you change places with your sister i like riding backwards i know what you like

00:12:50.680 --> 00:12:59.930
now do as i say bobby joe take billy's seat only one thing on her mind

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:11.980
Boys Bobby don't you ever get tired of his reading about love don't you ever want to do something about sure meet a man

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:16.540
like Robert Browning well where we never don't get much chance

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:25.030
traveling salesman gee I wish we lived in the city love can happen in place

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:28.850
Billy no there's a prince charming could come walking right through that door

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:33.700
Quite a load of supplies you put on, Kate.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:35.180
Uh, yeah, Floyd.

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:38.440
How much are you going to charge me for freighting all that up to my hotel?

00:13:38.780 --> 00:13:42.100
I reckon that's going to cost you two chicken dinners.

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:44.320
That's robbery.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:46.160
But I guess I'll have to pay it.

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:48.320
Isn't Floyd a car?

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:49.480
He sure is.

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:50.760
What's his last name?

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:52.560
Smoot, Floyd Smoot.

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:55.780
He and Charlie have been on this line for years.

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:58.790
They say they'll never stop running this old train.

00:13:59.070 --> 00:13:59.970
Well, I wouldn't bet on...

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:02.270
All right, folks, get your fares ready.

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:06.670
Oh, um, how much for me and my three daughters, Mr. Conductor?

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:09.510
Well, let's see. All over 12?

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:12.580
Mm, yeah, including me.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:19.020
That's gonna cost you two fresh baked apple pies.

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:20.500
What prices?

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:22.580
The railroad's gotta make a profit.

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:29.880
Say, if you want fresh apple pie, you better get up to the locomotive.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.920
and tell Betty Jo to stop the train at Ben Miller's Orchard.

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:34.020
Right you are, Kate.

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:37.580
Did I hear right you're gonna stop this train to pick apples?

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:39.840
Well, I kind of think we have to.

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:42.360
The tree's set a quarter mile from the track.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:46.140
This isn't a train, it's a rolling booby hatch.

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:06.370
Hi there. Mind if I have an apple?

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:07.700
Help yourself.

00:15:11.240 --> 00:15:16.680
Is this what you're after?

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.380
Oh, yes, ma'am. I've been just looking out the window there.

00:15:20.750 --> 00:15:23.050
Oh, I sure love this country. Everything's so green.

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:25.840
Oh, not as green as you think.

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:29.540
Well, I...

00:15:30.760 --> 00:15:31.760
Th-thanks for the apple.

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:43.730
Put your feet down. Sit up like a lady. There.

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:44.700
Almost where?

00:15:45.980 --> 00:15:47.360
My hotel, the Shady Rest.

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:48.560
I don't see any hotel.

00:15:49.090 --> 00:15:51.150
Sets up above the tracks on a little rise.

00:16:00.380 --> 00:16:02.520
Folks, there'll be a two- to three-hour layover here

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:04.500
whilst we go up to Kate's hotel for supper.

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:06.380
What do you mean, two- or three-hour layover?

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:07.640
According to this timetable,

00:16:07.900 --> 00:16:09.840
this train's not supposed to stop here at all.

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:11.740
